example.paragraph:
Let $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & {- 3} \\
      3 & 5 \\
      {- 1} & 7
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      2 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{c} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      2 \\
      5
      \end{bmatrix}$, and define a transformation $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{3}$ by $T(\mathbf{x}) = A\mathbf{x}$, so that

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,\mapsto\,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & 1 & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & 0
      \end{bmatrix}\,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}\, = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}$$

example.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 2 \\
      0 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. The transformation $\mathbf{T}:\text{ℝ}^{\text{2}}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ defined by $T(\mathbf{x}) = A\mathbf{x}$ is called a shear transformation. It can be shown that if $T$ acts on each point in the $2 \times 2$ square shown in Figure 4, then the set of images forms the sheared parallelogram. The key idea is to show that $T$ maps line segments onto line segments (as shown in Exercise 35) and then to check that the corners of the square map onto the vertices of the parallelogram. For instance, the image of the point $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is $T(\mathbf{u}) = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 2 \\
      0 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and the image of $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & 2 \\
      0 & 1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      6 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. $T$ deforms the square as if the top of the square were pushed to the right while the base is held fixed. Shear transformations appear in physics, geology, and crystallography.

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{cll}
      {T(c\mathbf{u} + d\mathbf{v})} & {= 3(c\mathbf{u} + d\mathbf{v})} & {\text{Definition~of~}T} \\
       & \left. \begin{array}{l}
      {= 3c\mathbf{u} + 3d\mathbf{v}} \\
      {= c(3\mathbf{u}) + d(3\mathbf{v})}
      \end{array}\quad \right\} & \text{Vector~arithmetic} \\
       & {= cT(\mathbf{u}) + dT(\mathbf{v})} & 
      \end{array}$$

example.paragraph:
Find the images under $T$ of $\mathbf{u} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      4 \\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{v} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      2 \\
      3
      \end{bmatrix}$, and $\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      6 \\
      4
      \end{bmatrix}$.

paragraph.math:
$$T(\mathbf{x}) = U\mathbf{x} = x_{1}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      .45 \\
      .25 \\
      .15
      \end{array} \right\rbrack + x_{2}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      .40 \\
      .30 \\
      .15
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      \text{Total cost of materials} \\
      \text{Total cost of labor} \\
      \text{Total cost of overhead}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$$

statement.paragraph:
Let $A = \,\,\mspace{-4mu}\begin{bmatrix}
      2 & 0 \\
      0 & 2
      \end{bmatrix}$, and define $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ by $T(\mathbf{x}) = A\mathbf{x}$. Find the images under $T$ of $\mathbf{u} = \,\mspace{-4mu}\,\,\mspace{-4mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{v} = \,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      a \\
      b
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Let $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      .5 & 0 & 0 \\
      0 & .5 & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & .5
      \end{bmatrix}$, $\mathbf{u} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{v} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      a \\
      b \\
      c
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Define $T:\text{ℝ}^{3}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{3}$ by $T(\mathbf{x}) = A\mathbf{x}$. Find $T(\mathbf{u})$ and $T(\mathbf{v})$.

Let $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ be a linear transformation that maps $\mathbf{u} = \,\,\mspace{-4mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ into $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and maps $\mathbf{v} = \,\,\mspace{-4mu}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ into $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Use the fact that $T$ is linear to find the images under $T$ of $3\mathbf{u}$, $2\mathbf{v}$, and $3\mathbf{u} + 2\mathbf{v}$.

Let $\mathbf{e}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{e}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{y}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{y}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      6
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and let $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ be a linear transformation that maps $\mathbf{e}_{1}$ into $\mathbf{y}_{1}$ and maps $\mathbf{e}_{2}$ into $\mathbf{y}_{2}$. Find the images of $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Let $\mathbf{x} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 2} \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      7 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and let $T:\text{ℝ}^{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2}$ be a linear transformation that maps $\mathbf{x}$ into $x_{1}\mathbf{v}_{1} + x_{2}\mathbf{v}_{2}$. Find a matrix $A$ such that $T(\mathbf{x})$ is $A\mathbf{x}$ for each $\mathbf{x}$.

[T] Let $\mathbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 7} \\
      {- 7} \\
      13 \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and let $A$ be the matrix in Exercise 46. Is $\mathbf{b}$ in the range of the transformation $\mathbf{x}\mapsto A{\mathbf{x}\text{?}}$ If so, find an $\mathbf{x}$ whose image under the transformation is $\mathbf{b}$.
